# MeCoil AWS deployment config.
#
# Copy this file to `infra/aws.env` and fill in the values. `infra/aws.env` is
# gitignored and must NEVER be committed.
#
# AWS CREDENTIALS ARE NOT STORED HERE. Export them in your terminal from the AWS
# console before running any `make aws-*` target, e.g.:
#     export AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=...
#     export AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=...
#     export AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=...        # if using temporary credentials
# The CDK/CLI pick these up automatically from the environment.

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# AWS region to deploy into.
AWS_REGION=eu-west-1

# The ONLY source IP allowed to reach SSH (port 22), as a bare IPv4 address.
# This is almost always your current public IP. Find it with:  curl -s https://checkip.amazonaws.com
# A /32 is appended automatically.
SSH_ALLOWED_IP=203.0.113.10

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# EC2 instance type (Graviton/arm64). Default t4g.micro is the cost-optimized pick.
INSTANCE_TYPE=t4g.micro

# Free dynamic DNS via DuckDNS (https://www.duckdns.org). The instance has no
# Elastic IP (that's billed 24/7); instead it gets a fresh public IP on every
# start and a boot-time script keeps <DUCKDNS_SUBDOMAIN>.duckdns.org pointed at
# it. Caddy then obtains a real Let's Encrypt certificate for that hostname.
#
# 1. Create a free account at https://www.duckdns.org and add a subdomain.
# 2. Set just the subdomain part here (not the full hostname):
#      DUCKDNS_SUBDOMAIN=mecoil
# 3. After the first `make aws-deploy`, copy your token from the DuckDNS
#    dashboard and run:  make aws-set-duckdns-token TOKEN=<your-token>
#
# Leave blank to skip DNS entirely — Caddy then serves a self-signed
# (internal CA) certificate on whatever the instance's current public IP is
# (check it with `make aws-status`).
DUCKDNS_SUBDOMAIN=
